# ── Line endings are part of the contract, not a preference ───────────────────
#
# AD-2 pins 119 report bodies by SHA-256 and requires them byte-identical. Those
# SHAs are computed over the file's BYTES (`read_to_string` -> strip front matter
# -> hash; no `\r` handling anywhere). Until 2026-08-29 nothing here constrained
# line endings, so git fell back to `core.autocrlf` — which is `true` on Windows
# and on GitHub's windows-latest runner.
#
# Measured, not assumed. Cloning this repo with `-c core.autocrlf=true` and
# reading one anchored report:
#     Windows checkout : 81 CRLF, 0 bare LF, 3103 bytes
#     LF checkout      :  0 CRLF,            3022 bytes
# Different bytes, different SHA. Every one of the 119 anchors fails on such a
# checkout, and `t1937_nine_strategy_anchors_unchanged` /
# `t1937b_canonical_strategy_anchors_unchanged` are exactly that failure observed
# on the windows-latest leg.
#
# The gate looked safe only because `verify_anchors.sh` runs on the ubuntu leg
# alone. A byte-immutability invariant that depends on which OS checked the repo
# out is not an invariant.

# Normalize everything to LF, in the repository AND in every working tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The evidence corpus is byte-immutable (AD-2): never translate it, in either
# direction, for any reason. Redundant with `eol=lf` above and deliberately so —
# this line states the intent at the files it protects.
evidence/** -text

# Vendored BMAD assets that legitimately ship with CRLF in their blobs. They are
# the only two tracked files in the repo containing CRLF; `text=auto` would
# rewrite them on the next commit that touches them.
.claude/skills/bmad-brainstorming/analysis/method-matrix.csv -text
.claude/skills/bmad-brainstorming/assets/brain-methods.csv   -text

# Binary assets (pre-existing).
crates/forecast/checkpoints/anchors/*.safetensors filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
crates/ui/tests/visual-baselines/** binary
